<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Java</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Nested aggregator for the Java client's three modules: one shared wrapper surface, two
        transports behind it (astubbs#242, docs/plans/2026-08-14-001-feat-language-proxy-plan.md).
        The api module owns the user-facing surface and its conformance suite; direct binds the
        engine in-process with no protobuf and no gRPC anywhere on its classpath (enforced in its
        pom); grpc speaks to the sidecar over the RPC hop. Seeded complete like the clients
        aggregator above it - the Java implementation work adds files inside these three modules,
        not entries here.

        Two modules were added to that seed, and neither is a client. Harness is a BUILD-GRAPH
        module: it holds the engine dependency, so no module a client wraps carries one. Demo is
        the reference demo of plan unit U35 - the runnable comparison every other language's demo
        is transcribed from, which lives beside the client library precisely so that it exercises
        it. Both are leaves nothing depends on, which is what lets them hold an engine edge safely.
        Each pom explains what that bought and how to measure it.
    -->
    <modules>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-api</module>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-direct</module>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-grpc</module>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-harness</module>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-demo</module>
    </modules>

    <!--
        THE gRPC GRAPH'S TWO PINS, DECLARED ONCE FOR THE TREE THAT NEEDS THEM. Only the modules that
        put gRPC on a classpath resolve either artifact, so grpc and harness inherit these and api
        and direct are unaffected - direct's bannedDependencies rule proves that, since a pin here
        adds nothing to a graph that has no gRPC in it.

        parallel-consumer-proxy's pom OWNS the reasoning for both and carries it in full. In one line
        each: gRPC drags error_prone_annotations in at several versions and resolution settles on the
        lowest, which the root pom's requireUpperBoundDeps refuses; and grpc-core brings gson 2.11.0
        at runtime, the version OSS Index matches for CVE-2025-53864 - an advisory about a library
        this repository does not depend on, pinned forward rather than excluded because an exclusion
        would carry a retirement condition nobody could ever discharge.

        The protocol module's error_prone pin is deliberately a version lower, since its highest
        requester differs; equalising the three would break one of them.
    -->
    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.google.errorprone</groupId>
                <artifactId>error_prone_annotations</artifactId>
                <version>2.48.0</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.google.code.gson</groupId>
                <artifactId>gson</artifactId>
                <version>2.14.0</version>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
                STATIC ANALYSIS FOR THE JAVA CLIENT, AND IT FAILS THE BUILD (astubbs#242; the policy
                and the per-language equivalents are in docs/client-static-analysis.md).

                Declared once here so every module beneath it inherits it - including any added
                later, which is the point of putting it here rather than in each child. The root pom already
                pluginManages this plugin - version, effort=Max, threshold=Medium - so nothing is
                repeated; what is added is the binding and the fact that it GATES.

                WHY process-classes AND NOT verify, which is the plugin's own default phase: the
                gating PR lane runs `bin/ci-unit-test.sh`, which stops at `test`. A verify-bound
                check would run only in the push-to-master full build - after review, on the wrong
                side of the merge - and would read as covered the whole time. process-classes is the
                first phase after compile, so `test`, `package` and `verify` all reach it, and so
                does `bin/build.sh -pl :<module> -am` on a laptop.

                WHY `check` AND NOT `spotbugs`: the `spotbugs` goal writes a report and exits 0.
                `check` reads that report and fails, which is the difference between analysis
                nobody reads and a gate. It runs the analysis itself (@Execute), so there is no
                second execution to keep in step.

                THIS IS A HARDER GATE THAN THE REPO-WIDE LANE, deliberately. maven.yml's
                `static: spotbugs` job is core-scoped, baseline-excluded and non-blocking - it
                reports what is NEW against master and never reddens. That design suits a codebase
                with 30 pre-existing findings (docs/inflight/static-spotbugs-latent-findings.md);
                these modules have none, and starting a new module at zero is the one moment a
                clean-slate gate is free.

                DISMISSING A FINDING: the shape that keeps the reason next to the code is
                @SuppressFBWarnings at the site, and it is unavailable here - the annotation needs
                com.github.spotbugs:spotbugs-annotations on the classpath, and the api module is
                dependency-free by design. So a dismissal goes in that module's own
                spotbugs-exclude.xml, named class by class and pattern by pattern with the reason
                written beside it. A module-wide or pattern-wide exclusion is the thing to refuse:
                it silences the finding for code nobody has written yet. Two modules need one today
                - api, for the zero-copy contract, and demo, whose output IS pre-rendered text; the
                rest are clean with no filter at all.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>com.github.spotbugs</groupId>
                <artifactId>spotbugs-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- version and configuration come from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>spotbugs-gate</id>
                        <phase>process-classes</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>check</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
